  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Supporting Basic Health Needs to Venezuelan Migrants in Darien Panama</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project aims to deliver immediate medical relief to migrants that have just crossed the Darien gap by providing self-administered first aid and dignity kits to targeted populations. Through this grant, IOM targeted intervention is in the Migrant Reception Center in Chiriqui, Panama, where Venezuelan migrants stop for several hours before moving on to their next destination. While waiting, IOM will distribute two sets of kits to migrants; a first aid kit containing self-administered over-the-counter essential medicines, including antibacterial ointment, and hygiene products, such as soap and lotion for burns and rashes, and a dignity kit for women that contains flashlights and menstrual pads. Additionally, an awareness-raising event hosted by the USA for IOM in Washington D.C. is planned to bring visibility to the worsening conditions, and increased migration flows through Darien. This event will bring together development organizations, private foundations, public partners, philanthropic individuals, and other key stakeholders to bring resources and sustainability to the health programs for migrants in Panama.</narrative>
  </description>
